1	# Textstats Implementation Plan
2	
3	> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use hyperpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or hyperpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.
4	
5	**Spec:** none (fixture)
6	
7	**Goal:** Build `textstats`, a small dependency-free Node.js module that reports four statistics about a piece of English prose: the number of words, the number of sentences, the most frequent words, and an estimated reading time.
8	
9	**Architecture:** One CommonJS module, `src/textstats.js`, exports four pure functions. A private `tokenize` helper (added in Task 1) is the single definition of what a "word" is; `wordCount`, `topWords` and `readingTimeMinutes` all build on it so the three numbers can never disagree about the text. `sentenceCount` (Task 2) uses its own terminator scan and borrows `tokenize` only to decide whether a segment holds any words at all. Every function validates its arguments and throws a `TypeError` or `RangeError` with a message naming the function, rather than returning a silent wrong answer. Each function has its own test file under `test/`, so a failing statistic points at one file.
10	
11	**Tech Stack:** Node.js 20 (`node:test`, `node:assert/strict`), CommonJS modules, no runtime or dev dependencies. The suite runs with `npm test`, which is `node --test`.
12	
13	## Conventions
14	
15	These apply to every task below. They are written down once so each task can stay short on process and long on content.
16	
17	- **Module format.** CommonJS (`require` / `module.exports`). `package.json` has no `type` field, and this plan never adds one.
18	- **Test files.** One file per function, named `test/<functionName>.test.js`, using `const { test } = require('node:test');` and `const assert = require('node:assert/strict');`. Test names are full sentences in the present tense that say what the function does ("counts hyphenated compounds as one word"), never "test 1".
19	- **Red before green.** Every task writes its test file first, runs `npm test`, and sees it fail for the stated reason before any implementation is written. A test that has never failed has not shown that it can.
20	- **Argument validation.** Text arguments must be strings. A non-string throws `TypeError` whose message starts with the function name and a colon. Numeric arguments that are out of range throw `RangeError` with the same prefix. Tests assert on the error class and on the message prefix, never on the full message text.
21	- **No state.** None of the functions mutates its arguments, caches results, or reads anything from the environment. Calling a function twice with the same arguments returns equal values.
22	- **Commits.** One commit per task, conventional prefix `feat:`, message in the imperative, and the test file and implementation in the same commit. Do not commit while `npm test` is red.
23	- **Scope.** English prose only. Right-to-left scripts, languages without spaces between words, and markup stripping are out of scope; the notes in each task name the specific cases that are deliberately left alone.
